mfortable but not guaranteed Republican hold", "Nixon has drawn high-profile progressive surrogates, but outside spending in the race has been minimal"], "rationale": "Surveys from early September through October put Moody ahead by one to eleven points, averaging near seven, and her cash advantage and statewide name recognition make an upset hard to see. Raters call the race Likely or Solid Republican and markets give Nixon roughly a one-in-ten chance, which matches the polling picture.", "watch": "If the closer pollsters, such as St. Pete Polls and Change Research, prove more accurate and the midterm environment sharpens further, the race could tighten into Lean R territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ashley Moody, appointed to Florida's Senate seat, enters the special election as a clear favorite over state Representative Angie Nixon. Surveys from late September and early October consistently show her ahead, with margins ranging from a two-point St. Pete Polls result to double-digit leads from Stetson and Targoz. Florida's Republican lean and Moody's profile as a longtime statewide officeholder frame the contest.\n\nMoney is lopsided: Moody has raised more than $11 million with $8.5 million on hand, while Nixon has raised roughly $975,000. Analysts across the spectrum rate the race Likely or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Nixon about a one-in-ten chance. Figures including Bernie Sanders have campaigned for Nixon, but outside spending has been minimal. The race looks likely to remain in Republican hands absent a major shift before Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1404, "margin": -5.31}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2259, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1936, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1439, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1034, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0984, "margin